# One line ending everywhere: LF. Hashes in release_manifest.json are over LF bytes, so a checkout that rewrites line endings
# (Windows autocrlf) would make every file look modified. setup.cmd and the PowerShell script use no labels and no multi-line
# constructs that need CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf
# Plugin files are kept exactly as their upstream release ships them: the plugin lock hashes those bytes, and some upstream
# manifests use CRLF (the Excalidraw one does), which a LF conversion would turn into a lock mismatch on every fresh clone.
.obsidian/plugins/*/manifest.json -text
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf
AI-Agent/vault-python text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.sqlite3 binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff2 binary
